Date: Thu Aug 20 12:23:51 2026
From: Michael Smith
Subject: R4.4 Fix StubGenerator.py dropping optional std::vector<T> parameter data
Source: COMCAST
Upstream-Status: Pending
Signed-off-by: Michael Smith <michael_smith2@comcast.com>

ProxyStubGenerator/StubGenerator.py generates broken COM-RPC stub
deserialization code for any interface method parameter typed
Core::OptionalType<std::vector<T>>. The generated reader loop pushes
each deserialized element into "<name>.Value()" instead of the local
"<name>Object__" builder variable, then unconditionally overwrites
"<name>" with the still-empty "<name>Object__" right after the loop.
The parameter ends up with IsSet() == true but an empty vector,
regardless of what the caller actually sent.

Confirmed against a live device build of entservices-voicecontrol's
IVoiceControl::SetVoiceInit, which has three parameters of this shape
(roles, capabilities, vrexFields): the caller sent a populated roles
array, but VoiceControlImplementation::SetVoiceInit received it as an
empty (but "set") vector, which was later rejected by the voice
server ("must not be empty invalidField=elements[1].roles").

The fix must be conditional on whether a fresh "<name>Object__"
builder variable was actually declared. For a top-level optional
vector parameter (not suppress_type), one is - it must be built up
directly and moved into the real parameter once at the end, so the
loop needs to target it directly. For an optional vector *field*
nested inside a struct (suppress_type, e.g. RemoteControl's
PairedRemoteInfo::wakeupCustomList), no separate builder variable is
ever declared - "<name>Object__" collapses back to the field's own
name, so the loop must keep targeting "<name>.Value()" as before, or
it tries to call reserve()/push_back() directly on the
Core::OptionalType wrapper, which doesn't compile. A first version of
this patch missed that distinction and broke that second case in CI:

  ProxyStubs_RemoteControl.cpp: error: 'class
  WPEFramework::Core::OptionalType<std::vector<unsigned int> >' has
  no member named 'reserve'/'push_back'

This version fixes that by only using the fresh builder variable when
one was actually declared, otherwise falling back to the original
".Value()" accessor. Verified by regenerating the ProxyStubs for both
IVoiceControl.h and IRemoteControl.h with this patch applied: the
VoiceControl fix (roles/capabilities/vrexFields build up the local
plain std::vector correctly) and RemoteControl's
PairedRemoteInfo::wakeupCustomList (nested optional vector field,
correctly still uses ".Value()", byte-identical to the pre-existing,
already-correct unpatched output) were both confirmed.

diff --git a/ProxyStubGenerator/StubGenerator.py b/ProxyStubGenerator/StubGenerator.py
index 6b5b73b..4925a25 100755
--- a/ProxyStubGenerator/StubGenerator.py
+++ b/ProxyStubGenerator/StubGenerator.py
@@ -1566,14 +1566,22 @@ def GenerateStubs2(output_file, source_file, project_dir, tree, ns, scan_only=Fa
                     length = EmitParam(interface, p.length, Normalize("%sSize" % obj_name))
                     ReadParameter(length)
                     CheckRange(p, ("%s" % length.as_rvalue))
-                    emit.Line("%s.reserve(%s);" % (p.as_rvalue, length.as_rvalue))
+                    # When a fresh obj_name builder was declared above (not suppress_type),
+                    # it must be built up directly and moved into p.name once at the end -
+                    # p.as_rvalue (p.name/".Value()") is a *different* object in that case,
+                    # so using it here would silently drop every element that gets read.
+                    # When no fresh builder was declared (suppress_type, e.g. an optional
+                    # vector field inside a struct), obj_name is just p.name again, so
+                    # p.as_rvalue (its ".Value()" accessor) is the correct - and only - target.
+                    builder = obj_name if not p.suppress_type else p.as_rvalue
+                    emit.Line("%s.reserve(%s);" % (builder, length.as_rvalue))
 
                     index = chr(ord('i') + p.name.count('Item'))
                     element = EmitParam(interface, p.element, Normalize(obj_name + "Item"), parent=p)
                     emit.Line("for (%s %s = 0; %s < %s; %s++) {" % (p.length.type_name, index, index, length.as_rvalue, index))
                     emit.IndentInc()
                     ReadParameter(element)
-                    emit.Line("%s.push_back(std::move(%s));" % (p.as_rvalue, element.as_rvalue))
+                    emit.Line("%s.push_back(std::move(%s));" % (builder, element.as_rvalue))
                     emit.IndentDec()
                     emit.Line("}")
 
